<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_union.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-union">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-union">
  <primary>UNION</primary>
 </indexterm>
 <indexterm zone="cypher-union">
  <primary>INTERSECT</primary>
 </indexterm>
 <indexterm zone="cypher-union">
  <primary>EXCEPT</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>UNION</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>UNION</refname>
  <refpurpose>combine the results of two or more queries into one</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
<replaceable class="parameter">query</replaceable> { UNION | INTERSECT | EXCEPT } [ ALL | DISTINCT ] <replaceable class="parameter">query</replaceable> [ ... ]
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-union-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   A set operator joins two complete queries into one result.
   <command>UNION</command> returns the rows in either query.
   <command>INTERSECT</command> returns the rows in both.
   <command>EXCEPT</command> returns the rows in the first query and not in the second.
   The three are one construct with three operations, and every rule below applies to
   all of them. Each operand is a complete query with its own
   <xref linkend="cypher-return"/>. An operand cannot end in a write clause and cannot be
   a bare <xref linkend="cypher-match"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Every operand must return the same number of columns. Columns are matched by
   position, not by name, so the names need not agree. The result takes its names from
   the first query. A query with a different number of columns is refused with
   <literal>each UNION query must have the same number of columns</literal>, which names
   <literal>UNION</literal> for all three operators.
  </para>

  <para>
   Columns matched by position must also have a type in common. A Cypher result column
   is jsonb, so operands normally meet without any work. A column that carries a type of
   its own, such as a read of a promoted property, is converted to jsonb to meet a jsonb
   column. A vertex, an edge and a path have no such conversion, so a graph element
   column meets only a column of the same graph type.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>DISTINCT</literal> is the default: duplicate rows are removed.
   <literal>UNION</literal> and <literal>UNION DISTINCT</literal> are the same statement.
   <literal>ALL</literal> keeps the duplicates and skips the work of removing them. The
   operators chain, and a chain of one operator is evaluated left to right.
  </para>

  <para>
   Row order in the result is not defined. An <xref linkend="cypher-orderby"/>,
   <xref linkend="cypher-skip"/> or <xref linkend="cypher-limit"/> written after a set
   operation does not sort or page the combined rows, as the notes below show. A set
   operation may stand on either side of <xref linkend="cypher-next"/>, and that is the
   way to sort or page what the operation produced: the query right of
   <literal>NEXT</literal> receives the combined rows. Wrapping the set operation in a
   <xref linkend="cypher-call"/> subquery body, or in a SQL <literal>SELECT</literal>,
   does the same job.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-union-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">query</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A complete query ending in a <command>RETURN</command> with the same number of
      columns as every other query in the chain.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>ALL</literal></term>
    <term><literal>DISTINCT</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <literal>ALL</literal> keeps duplicate rows. <literal>DISTINCT</literal> removes
      them, and is the default, so writing it changes nothing.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-union-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Everyone connected to <literal>Inception</literal>, whichever way they are connected:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person)-[:directed]-&gt;(:movie {title: 'Inception'}) RETURN p.name AS name
UNION
MATCH (p:person)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(:movie {title: 'Inception'}) RETURN p.name AS name;

        name
---------------------
 "Christopher Nolan"
 "Leonardo DiCaprio"
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>UNION ALL</literal> keeps every row from both operands and skips the work of
   removing duplicates. The two rows here are distinct anyway, so
   <literal>ALL</literal> shows only in the cost:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {genre: 'Adventure'}) RETURN m.title AS film
UNION ALL
MATCH (m:movie) WHERE m.year = 2021 RETURN m.title AS film;

            film
----------------------------
 "Jurassic Park"
 "The Matrix Resurrections"
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The rows in both queries:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {genre: 'Action'}) RETURN m.title AS film
INTERSECT
MATCH (m:movie) WHERE m.year &lt; 1995 RETURN m.title AS film;

             film
------------------------------
 "The Terminator"
 "Terminator 2: Judgment Day"
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The people who directed something but never acted:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person)-[:directed]-&gt;(m:movie) RETURN p.name AS name
EXCEPT
MATCH (p:person)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie) RETURN p.name AS name
NEXT ORDER BY name RETURN name;

        name
---------------------
 "Andrew Niccol"
 "Christopher Nolan"
 "Denis Villeneuve"
 "James Cameron"
 "Joss Whedon"
 "Lana Wachowski"
 "Lilly Wachowski"
 "Luc Besson"
 "Neill Blomkamp"
 "Paul Verhoeven"
 "Ridley Scott"
 "Steven Spielberg"
(12 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Sort and page the combined rows with <command>NEXT</command>:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) WHERE m.genre = 'Adventure' RETURN m.title AS film
UNION
MATCH (m:movie) WHERE m.genre = 'Sci-Fi' RETURN m.title AS film
NEXT ORDER BY film LIMIT 3 RETURN film;

        film
---------------------
 "Avatar"
 "Blade Runner"
 "Blade Runner 2049"
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-union-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   An <command>ORDER BY</command>, <command>SKIP</command> or <command>LIMIT</command>
   written after a set operation attaches to the last query in the chain, not to the
   combined result. It sorts or pages that one operand before the sets are combined, so
   the <literal>LIMIT 1</literal> here does not produce one row:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) WHERE m.genre = 'Adventure' RETURN m.title AS film
UNION
MATCH (m:movie) WHERE m.genre = 'Sci-Fi' RETURN m.title AS film
LIMIT 1;

      film
-----------------
 "Inception"
 "Jurassic Park"
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   One row survived the <command>LIMIT</command> in the Sci-Fi operand, which returns ten
   films without it, and the single Adventure film joined that row afterwards. Name
   resolution follows the same rule, so such a sort key has to name a column of the last
   operand:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) RETURN m.title AS first_col
UNION
MATCH (p:person) RETURN p.name AS second_col
ORDER BY first_col LIMIT 2;

ERROR:  variable does not exist
LINE 4: ORDER BY first_col LIMIT 2;
                 ^
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-union-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   The three operators implement the set operator of ISO/IEC 39075:2024, clause 14.2:
   <literal>UNION</literal>, <literal>EXCEPT</literal> or <literal>INTERSECT</literal>
   with an optional set quantifier, <literal>DISTINCT</literal> implicit when none is
   given. AgensGraph implements all three operators and both quantifiers, features GQ03
   through GQ07. The standard's <literal>OTHERWISE</literal> conjunction, which takes the
   second query's rows only when the first returns none, is not implemented. The standard
   also applies the order by and page statement of clause 14.9 to the combined result,
   while AgensGraph attaches it to the last query of the chain.
  </para>

  <para>
   openCypher has only <command>UNION</command> and <literal>UNION ALL</literal>. Both
   are accepted here unchanged. <command>INTERSECT</command> and
   <command>EXCEPT</command>, and the explicit <literal>DISTINCT</literal> quantifier,
   are available in AgensGraph and not in openCypher.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

